 "statements": {
  "ampere": "Since current is a charge divided by a time, its SI unit is one coulomb per second, named the ampere for the French physicist André-Marie Ampère. Fuses, circuit breakers and many electrical appliances carry ratings in amperes.",
  "ohm": "The unit of resistance is one volt per ampere, which is named the ohm and written with the capital Greek omega, $1\\;\\Omega = 1\\;\\text{V/A}$. An object that has simple resistance is called a resistor, even when its resistance is small.",
  "resistivity": "For a given shape, the resistance depends on the material the object is made of, and the resistivity is the property that carries that dependence. Resistivity is an intrinsic property of a material, independent of its shape or size, and its unit is the ohm-meter.",
  "electric-power": "Electric power is the rate at which electrical energy is supplied by a source or dissipated by a device. Since electric energy is $\\text{PE} = qV$, the power is the charge per second times the voltage, which is the current times the voltage, $P = IV$. One ampere-volt is one watt."
 },
